1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,917
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,917 --> 00:00:07,021
This is chapter nineteen: HashMap internals.

3
00:00:07,471 --> 00:00:13,453
HashMap is the most used data structure in enterprise Java, and the most misunderstood.

4
00:00:13,453 --> 00:00:16,891
Most developers say it's O of one, and stop there.

5
00:00:16,891 --> 00:00:26,586
That answer misses the production incidents: maps that silently lose entries, lookups that degrade to scans, and rehashing storms under load.

6
00:00:27,486 --> 00:00:29,546
A HashMap is an array of bins.

7
00:00:29,546 --> 00:00:31,881
It starts with the key's hashCode.

8
00:00:32,331 --> 00:00:37,237
That hash is spread: the high sixteen bits are mixed into the low sixteen.

9
00:00:37,687 --> 00:00:43,085
Then the index is computed with a bitmask, table length minus one, AND the hash.

10
00:00:43,535 --> 00:00:50,511
And the bin at that index is either empty, a short linked list, or, under heavy collision, a red-black tree.

11
00:00:50,961 --> 00:00:53,532
Five constants govern its behaviour.

12
00:00:53,532 --> 00:00:56,604
Default capacity sixteen, allocated lazily.

13
00:00:56,604 --> 00:00:58,676
Load factor point seven five.

14
00:00:58,676 --> 00:01:07,319
Bins become trees at eight entries and turn back into lists at six, so a bin at the boundary doesn't flip back and forth.

15
00:01:07,319 --> 00:01:12,891
And below a table length of sixty-four, the map grows instead of making trees.

16
00:01:13,791 --> 00:01:16,821
Here's the hash function from the JDK source.

17
00:01:17,271 --> 00:01:19,283
A null key hashes to zero.

18
00:01:19,283 --> 00:01:24,780
That's why a HashMap supports exactly one null key, always in bin zero.

19
00:01:25,230 --> 00:01:31,843
The index is a bitmask, and for a sixteen-slot table it only looks at the bottom four bits.

20
00:01:31,843 --> 00:01:37,730
A hashCode that varies only in its high bits would put every key in the same bin.

21
00:01:37,730 --> 00:01:41,364
So the high bits are XORed down into the low ones.

22
00:01:41,364 --> 00:01:42,745
One shift, one XOR.

23
00:01:43,195 --> 00:01:50,250
And that bitmask is a cheap modulo that only works because the table length is always a power of two.

24
00:01:50,250 --> 00:01:53,115
Ask for seventeen and you get thirty-two.

25
00:01:54,015 --> 00:01:58,683
When a bin reaches eight nodes, the map considers making it a tree.

26
00:01:58,683 --> 00:02:10,669
But below a table length of sixty-four, it resizes instead, because a long bin in a small table more likely means the table is too small than that the hash function is bad.

27
00:02:10,669 --> 00:02:13,108
Inside a tree bin, lookup is log k.

28
00:02:13,558 --> 00:02:21,759
That's the honest complexity statement: O of one on average, and O of log n in the worst case, since Java 8.

29
00:02:21,759 --> 00:02:32,011
The old O of n answer describes Java 7, when attackers could send thousands of colliding keys and turn every lookup into a linear scan.

30
00:02:32,911 --> 00:02:37,855
When size passes the threshold, capacity doubles and every entry moves.

31
00:02:37,855 --> 00:02:40,293
Java 8 does this with a neat trick.

32
00:02:40,743 --> 00:02:50,833
Because capacity is a power of two and exactly doubles, each entry either stays at the same index, or moves by exactly the old capacity.

33
00:02:51,283 --> 00:02:54,473
A single bit of the cached hash decides which.

34
00:02:54,473 --> 00:03:02,099
So each bin splits into a low list and a high list in one pass, with no rehashing, and the order is preserved.

35
00:03:02,549 --> 00:03:04,862
That order preservation matters.

36
00:03:04,862 --> 00:03:13,174
Java 7 reversed the lists, and two threads resizing at once could build a cycle that spun at a hundred percent CPU.

37
00:03:13,174 --> 00:03:17,944
Java 8 removed that failure, but HashMap is still not thread-safe.

38
00:03:17,944 --> 00:03:20,619
Concurrent writes still lose updates.

39
00:03:21,519 --> 00:03:25,619
This is the HashMap bug that reaches production most often.

40
00:03:25,619 --> 00:03:29,303
Put an entry with a mutable key, then change the key.

41
00:03:29,303 --> 00:03:34,585
The hash changes, but the entry stays in the bin computed from the old hash.

42
00:03:34,585 --> 00:03:39,589
So get returns null, containsKey returns false, but size still says one.

43
00:03:39,589 --> 00:03:43,620
The entry is unreachable, unremovable, and leaked forever.

44
00:03:44,070 --> 00:03:48,778
So keys must be immutable in every field used by hashCode and equals.

45
00:03:48,778 --> 00:03:51,985
Records are the cleanest way to guarantee that.

46
00:03:52,885 --> 00:03:55,620
Size the map when you know the count.

47
00:03:55,620 --> 00:04:02,420
Building a two-hundred-thousand-entry catalogue from a default map triggers fifteen resizes.

48
00:04:02,420 --> 00:04:07,668
Java 19's HashMap dot newHashMap sizes it so all entries fit with none.

49
00:04:07,668 --> 00:04:12,917
Remember, the plain constructor takes a capacity, not an expected size.

50
00:04:13,367 --> 00:04:18,095
And use merge and computeIfAbsent instead of get, check, put.

51
00:04:18,095 --> 00:04:22,203
Merge collapses duplicate SKUs by summing quantities.

52
00:04:22,203 --> 00:04:25,847
computeIfAbsent groups orders by delivery slot.

53
00:04:26,297 --> 00:04:34,538
Compare putIfAbsent with a new ArrayList, which allocates a list on every single call and throws most of them away.

54
00:04:34,538 --> 00:04:38,265
The computeIfAbsent lambda only runs the first time.

55
00:04:39,165 --> 00:04:43,704
Every structural change bumps a modCount, and iterators check it.

56
00:04:43,704 --> 00:04:50,407
So removing entries inside a for-each over the key set throws a ConcurrentModificationException.

57
00:04:50,857 --> 00:04:55,337
Use removeIf on the entry set, or an explicit iterator's remove.

58
00:04:55,787 --> 00:05:02,014
And remember, fail-fast is a best-effort bug detector, not a synchronisation mechanism.

59
00:05:02,014 --> 00:05:04,662
Never write logic that depends on it.

60
00:05:05,562 --> 00:05:11,568
Each entry is a Node object: a header, the cached hash, and three references.

61
00:05:11,568 --> 00:05:15,311
That's thirty-two bytes on a typical 64-bit JVM.

62
00:05:15,761 --> 00:05:20,881
Add the table array, about five bytes per entry at the default load factor.

63
00:05:21,331 --> 00:05:26,771
Java 25's compact object headers shrink the Node to twenty-four bytes.

64
00:05:27,221 --> 00:05:34,885
So a million-entry map costs around thirty-seven megabytes of overhead before a single key or value object.

65
00:05:34,885 --> 00:05:37,248
On map-heavy heaps, that adds up.

66
00:05:38,148 --> 00:05:39,521
Production rules.

67
00:05:39,521 --> 00:05:43,558
Size maps with newHashMap when you know the count.

68
00:05:43,558 --> 00:05:45,172
Use records as keys.

69
00:05:45,172 --> 00:05:47,837
Prefer merge and computeIfAbsent.

70
00:05:47,837 --> 00:05:50,824
Return Map dot copyOf from accessors.

71
00:05:50,824 --> 00:05:52,600
Declare fields as Map.

72
00:05:52,600 --> 00:05:55,023
And use EnumMap for enum keys.

73
00:05:55,473 --> 00:05:59,859
Never share a HashMap across threads without safe publication.

74
00:05:59,859 --> 00:06:01,486
Never use mutable keys.

75
00:06:01,486 --> 00:06:07,216
Don't tune the load factor without measuring, because the default is well chosen.

76
00:06:07,216 --> 00:06:09,338
Never rely on iteration order.

77
00:06:09,338 --> 00:06:13,582
Avoid containsValue in hot paths, since it scans everything.

78
00:06:13,582 --> 00:06:17,190
And never modify the map inside a compute function.

79
00:06:18,090 --> 00:06:24,831
Java 8 brought treeified bins, the order-preserving resize, and merge, compute and getOrDefault.

80
00:06:25,281 --> 00:06:35,756
Java 9 and 10 added Map dot of and copyOf, and made recursive updates inside computeIfAbsent throw instead of silently corrupting the map.

81
00:06:36,206 --> 00:06:38,745
Java 19 added newHashMap.

82
00:06:39,195 --> 00:06:41,332
Java 21 added SequencedMap.

83
00:06:41,332 --> 00:06:51,781
And here's a correction to common notes: it applies to LinkedHashMap and TreeMap, not HashMap, because HashMap has no defined order.

84
00:06:52,231 --> 00:06:56,690
And Java 25's compact headers shrink each Node by a quarter.

85
00:06:57,590 --> 00:06:58,232
To recap.

86
00:06:58,232 --> 00:07:07,511
The hash is spread, masked to an index, and lands in a bin that's a list, or a tree once it reaches eight in a large enough table.

87
00:07:07,511 --> 00:07:12,079
Lookups are O of one on average and log n at worst since Java 8.

88
00:07:12,079 --> 00:07:16,646
Resizing splits each bin by a single bit, so size maps up front.

89
00:07:16,646 --> 00:07:20,929
And keys must be immutable, or entries get stranded forever.

90
00:07:21,379 --> 00:07:24,526
Next is chapter twenty: sets, queues and deques.

91
00:07:24,526 --> 00:07:25,837
Thanks for watching.
